'''wattle-gum''' (wot′l-gum), ''n.'' An Australian gum. See ''gum arabic'', under ''gum''<sup>2</sup>.

'''wattle-jaws''' (wot′l-jâz), ''n. pl.'' Long, lanky jaws; lantern-jaws. ''Halliwell.''

'''wattle-tree''' (wot′l-trē), ''n.'' Same as ''wattle'', 7.

{{smaller block|The golden blossoms of the ''wattle-trees'' mark the period [spring] everywhere in Australia.
{{right|''Contemporary Rev.'', LII. 407.}}}}

'''wattle-turkey''' (wot′l-tėr″ki), ''n.'' The brush-turkey, ''Talegallus lathami.'' See cut under ''Talegallus.''

'''wattlework''' (wot′l-wėrk), ''n.'' A wattled fabric or structure; wickerwork.

{{smaller block|A nest of ''wattle-work'' formed of silver wire.
{{right|''S. K. Cat. Sp. Ex.'', 1862.}}
The huts were probably more generally made of ''wattle-work'', like those of the Swiss lakes.
{{right|''Dawkins'', Early Man in Britain, p. 271.}}}}

'''wattling''' (wot′ling), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''wattle'', ''v.''] A construction made by interweaving twigs, osiers, or flat and elastic material of any sort, with stakes or rods as a substructure.

{{smaller block|The houses . . . have here 2 or 3 partitions on the ground floor, made with a ''watling'' of canes or sticks.}}

